00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.270
Let's get started.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:13.450
So how is securing a container
different from securing a VM?

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:14.820
Let's go over a few areas.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.360
First, surface of attack--

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.060
containers package all the
dependencies and application

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:24.170
needs into a nice
little package so

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.800
that you don't need as many
dependencies, libraries,

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.220
processes running
on the host image

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.340
to support the
containerized applications.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.580
This way, you're able
to minimize the host OS

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.680
and reduce your
attack surface there.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.430
For resource
isolation, containers

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.380
has that built in as well.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.790
It uses namespaces and C
groups to allocate resources

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.450
for particular resources,
such as a storage

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:58.850
volume to a containerized
application or process.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.370
For permissions,
just like VMs, you

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:04.520
want to configure fine grained
permissions with container

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.470
based applications as well.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:09.050
But one big difference
there is containers

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.060
encourage microservices.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.690
So instead of running one
giant monolithic application,

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.060
it has access to everything.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.450
Now it's much easier to have
smaller applications having

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.700
smaller access scope as well.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:27.670
So it's much easier to implement
a fine grained access control.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.220
And then, last but
not least, lifecycle--

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.860
this is probably the
biggest difference

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:34.870
between containerized
applications

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.180
and VM based applications.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.400
With containers, it's immutable.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.470
It encourages fast deployment.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.710
It's very developer friendly.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:48.280
So now we see applications
getting released daily,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.630
versus in the VM based
world, oftentimes,

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:56.050
a monolithic application doesn't
get updated for a few months.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.110
So with this and
with microservices,

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.440
you have a lot more services to
keep track of in the production

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.060
environment, a lot
more deployment

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.450
to keep track of in the
production development.

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.890
A lot of people adopt the
continuous integration

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:13.300
and deployment model
to continue pushing out

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.150
new changes over time.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.440
So for a lot of
organizations, the last piece,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.970
the lifecycle piece,
is where most changes

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.530
needs to happen to
the infrastructure

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.750
because now you have
continuous deployments.

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:29.090
We have to streamline
your security checks.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:32.200
And so that's going to be
the area of today's focus

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:33.680
for this talk.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.560
We're going to share a few
tools, lessons, container

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.900
characteristics to
help you navigate

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:46.210
this migration to container
based application lifecycle

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.390
management.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:53.550
So containers has a
few characteristics

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:57.300
that is going to enable you
to have a different security

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:01.560
mindset and implement a
different type of security

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.400
model that leads to better
security in the long run.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.250
Let's go through them.

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:10.620
The first, containers are
short lived and frequently

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.060
redeployed.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:15.480
This means that you can have
one centralized process that

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.360
apply changes to your
production workload.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.050
And there will be less
need for direct access

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.930
to the running applications
to make any on the fly changes

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.960
because making a change
through a centralized pipeline

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.790
is as simple as
click of a button.

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:34.980
So you will be able to
minimize ad hoc changes

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.820
and have a unified
process for apply changes

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:42.150
to the production environment.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:43.920
Containers are also immutable.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.130
So instead of making
one-line changes

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:51.600
to a containerized
application, the intention

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.270
is to make changes to
the container as a whole.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.730
You change one line of
code, you click a button,

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.217
and it reviews the entire
thing and redeploys

00:04:59.217 --> 00:05:00.300
to production environment.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.760
Now you have something
brand new running.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.320
And the Kubernetes
infrastructure

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.670
allows deployment that
causes minimum interruption.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:12.030
So you're able to apply
changes continuously.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:19.210
No more Saturday 2:00 AM update
Windows necessary anymore.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.590
Containers also enable
fast paced development.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:27.155
This is oftentimes the biggest
tool to the container format

00:05:27.155 --> 00:05:29.690
is that developers are able
to test out and push out

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.090
new changes super fast.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:36.020
As the security stakeholders
and compliance stakeholders,

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.160
now every release
takes days to complete,

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.770
versus it used to take
months to complete.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.140
So we cannot afford to
scrutinize every release

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.850
anymore-- take weeks to examine
every components of each

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:50.870
release.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:54.130
This means that streamlined
security controls are now

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:54.670
necessary.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:58.570
It needs to be baked into the
automated release processes

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:00.370
in your CI/CD pipeline.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.890
It gets applied on the
fly so that developers

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:07.190
don't have to slow down and
wait for the security checks

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:13.450
to happen before new changes
get applied into production.

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:15.130
The good news is
the container format

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:17.690
is declarative and respectable.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:22.120
It means that you don't have
to run the containers in order

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.520
to examine the content of it.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.420
It allows you to
have more visibility

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:31.510
into the contents of a
container before you deploy it,

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:32.840
before you run it.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:36.490
It also means that you can
have much better automation

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:40.750
on security controls
and scans to automate

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:46.117
those type of checks as
part of your CI/CD pipeline.

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:54.430
However, we have here
from a number of users

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:59.140
that taking care of container
security can be manual.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.820
For most container
users that we talked to,

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.920
they have adopted
continuous integration

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.470
and continuous
deployment because it

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:08.930
is so developer friendly--
apply changes super quickly.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:10.390
But a lot of them
are still stuck

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:13.600
in the old security model
where deploy controls are

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.250
applied manually.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:19.480
We have stakeholders
basically manually making

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:26.030
sure that every release has met
all the necessary requirement.

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:27.550
It's also ad hoc.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:31.060
The old habit is hard
to break, and we also

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:33.220
see that admins
and developers are

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:36.010
accessing running containers
directly and making changes

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:37.180
on the fly.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:40.990
And this opens doors to
changes that are not tracked

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:42.640
in a centralized process.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:50.680
And you have running
code that diverts

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:55.480
from its original deploy states,
and you can't explain or track

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.870
that using your centralized
processing pipeline.

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:03.800
And those kind of changes
happen at the end of a release.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.580
It's a more reactive
model instead

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:08.570
of a preventative model.

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:12.610
So instead of controlling
what gets into a production

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:15.160
by making those ad
hoc manual changes,

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:18.280
now you're reacting
to bad things that

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:20.920
are happening in the production
environment in a very

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:23.380
manual way.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:27.570
So as I mentioned,
having a CI/CD pipeline

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:31.530
does not stop untrusted code
from being deployed, right?

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:34.770
You can have a CI/CD
pipeline that has scans

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:37.679
and analysis enabled
and all the goodness.

00:08:37.679 --> 00:08:40.840
But you can have a
privileged attacker.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:43.440
It could be a developer
who is making a mistake.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.900
It could also be a
compromised account

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:49.540
that is able to deploy code
directly to production.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:52.620
And now this person is bypassing
all the checks that you have.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.230
You no longer have total
control of what goes

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:56.713
into production environment.

00:08:56.713 --> 00:08:59.130
And on top of that, if you
don't lock down your production

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:02.250
environment, you have
admins and developers

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.890
and privileged personnel
having direct access

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:06.360
to the running jobs.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:09.420
And now you have another
vector of untracked changes

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:12.400
to your production environment.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.530
And even for those
who have a lock down

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:18.790
or have a centralized
CI/CD, there

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:21.960
are many common pitfalls
that could happen.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:25.300
In the developer phase,
people could make a mistake.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:28.840
They could check in code that
contains vulnerabilities.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:30.940
And their icon could be phished.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:33.970
Or there could be a
malicious insider.

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:40.900
During the CI/CD phase, if
you have one monolithic,

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:43.060
super-privileged
robot account that's

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:46.660
running the entire
end-to-end CI/CD process,

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:49.330
you may experience a
confused deputy problem.

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:53.560
Your CI/CD pipeline may have
a bug in the testing phase,

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:57.850
and it gets tricked into
reading your source data

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:03.340
and pushing random images
to your image repository.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:04.840
There could be
privileged escalation

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:06.880
there in the pipeline itself.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:09.340
And in the deploy
stage, you could also

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:13.200
have trusted account that
is able to bypass the CI/CD

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:15.490
checks.

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:18.220
Having a CI/CD, again,
does not prevent

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.590
deploying on trusted code.

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:22.790
So what to do?

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:26.320
We have a few best
practices here, hopefully,

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:29.960
that can help you navigate
around those common pitfalls.

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:33.550
First, you should have a
centralized, locked-down CI/CD

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:37.330
process as a single choke
point for applying changes

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:39.570
to your production environment.

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:44.000
You should build images
from only trusted sources,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:47.810
and you should also have
streamlined scanning

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:49.760
and analysis for images.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:54.230
Last but not least, explicitly
verify each of the images

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:57.170
that you deploy to your
production environment

00:10:57.170 --> 00:11:00.990
and have that baking as
part of your CI/CD process.

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:03.740
I'm going to go through
each of the best practices

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:08.680
and look into the details
of what exactly it requires.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:14.180
But first, having a centralized,
locked-down CI/CD pipeline.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:19.850
It is very beneficial
to have a single process

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:24.720
that every changes to your
production have to go through.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:27.780
It makes it easier
to track changes.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:31.020
It makes it easier to explain
to your stakeholders what

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:34.970
are the criteria for
production release is.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:38.050
We also encourage that
you should implement least

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:40.120
privilege for each
of the release step.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:43.300
For example, the CI
portion of the pipeline

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:44.950
should have a
different permission

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:47.530
from the CD portion
of the pipeline.

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:50.590
So that the stage that
is able to deploy code

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:53.680
to your production
environment shouldn't also

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:55.910
have permission to
checking code, for example,

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:58.570
to your repository, or
read your source code,

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:01.670
or checking your source code.

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:04.790
Don't blindly trust
your image repository.

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:09.290
Just because an image is checked
into your production repository

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:11.700
does not mean that it
can be safely deployed

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:13.370
to your production environment.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:16.070
It could contain
vulnerabilities.

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:18.030
It could be outdated.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:21.260
It could be from
something from a year ago.

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:24.590
You really want to verify
every image you deploy

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:29.120
to your production explicitly
at the time of the deployment.

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:32.000
You should also minimize ad hoc
accesses to running containers.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.400
So there's no good having
a centralized process

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:36.830
if people can access your
running jobs directly.

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:39.750
And you need to lock
down the entire process.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:42.320
There are also a few
recommended practices

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:47.400
that I wouldn't go into
details, such as having metadata

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.330
so you can examine later.

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:53.270
And it just means that
automated does not

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:55.410
mean the human should
not be involved.

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:57.560
It's always good to
have human checks

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:01.280
as part of your CI/CD pipeline.

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:05.870
Next lesson, build images
from trusted sources.

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:09.500
So we know that we'll look
at our food, and we're like,

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:11.090
is this organic?

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:12.350
Is this FDA approved?

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:14.370
Similarly, when you
build your content

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:18.980
or you want to make sure you
use minimum secure base images

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:23.590
so that you minimize your attack
surface from the beginning.

00:13:23.590 --> 00:13:25.620
You want to whitelist
only trusted code

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:28.440
from your repositories
or trusted third party

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:33.630
repositories so that you don't
have random images downloaded

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:38.090
from the internet making their
way into your production job.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:41.490
You should also be explicit in
your step in your Docker file,

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:46.190
which means that use explicit
version to not just tag

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:48.920
explicit version of
what it's scripting.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:53.510
So when you run a Docker
viewed two days from now,

00:13:53.510 --> 00:13:54.920
it would produce the same thing.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.080
When you have a
problem, and you're

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.480
able to go back and
examine exactly what

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:04.960
it was pulled into a container.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:07.890
So to help with
that best practice,

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:11.700
Google has a managed
base image product.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:16.810
It is available in a number
of common distributions,

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:19.540
such as Debian, Ubuntu, CentOS.

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:21.810
It is hosted on
cloud marketplace.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:23.610
These basic images
are maintained

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:26.550
by Google, used by
Google internally

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:29.400
on a number of our
infrastructures and products.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:30.600
So keep them up to date.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.690
We apply vulnerability
scannings to them constantly.

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:36.120
Whenever there is
available fixes,

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:37.650
we apply the latest patch.

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:40.620
So if you have
continuous integration,

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:44.790
new images get built of using
one of the managed secure base

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:45.600
images.

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:49.260
You would always pull the most
recent up to date base images

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:50.610
as managed by us.

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:53.790
This is a very convenient
way and a low effort way

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:58.480
to start from a clean
slate on your build side.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:02.040
Lesson number three,
streamlined tests and analysis--

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:05.560
so as I mentioned earlier,
having a CI/CD is great.

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:09.370
But it's better if you have
scannings, or vettings,

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:12.880
or testings baked into
that so that only code

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:16.390
that have passed those
criterias makes their way

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:18.860
into production.

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:22.610
So simple things to do are
enable image vulnerability

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:26.030
scanning and have
automated integration test

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:28.410
stages in your CI/CD pipeline.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:31.400
And it's always good to
have a QA sign-off, which

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:34.880
could be a human
involved step, or it

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:39.440
could be a manual
integration as a gatekeeper.

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:44.120
And there are also some
open source software

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:46.280
that we provide to
help you implement

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:49.040
additional structured
tests or image diff

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:52.940
to further validate the
images in your CI/CD pipeline.

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:57.530
To help with this lesson,
we have a container registry

00:15:57.530 --> 00:16:00.530
vulnerability scanning
product that is available

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:03.240
today in beta.

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:07.380
What it does is every time-- so
when you enable this feature,

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:10.950
it looks at every
single image that you

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.110
check into your
Container Registry

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:14.740
to look for known
vulnerabilities

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:19.870
in a number of common base
images, Debian, Alpine, Ubuntu.

00:16:19.870 --> 00:16:26.470
It's constantly contacting the
industry standard vulnerability

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:30.430
database to pull the most
recent vulnerabilities used

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:31.850
for analysis.

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:33.970
So every time there
is a new vulnerability

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:37.060
becomes available, it will
be taking into consideration

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:40.780
for the scans that the product
performs on your images

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:43.280
in the repository.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:45.750
It also plugs it into
your existing tool chain,

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:47.730
so it publishes the
result. And then

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:50.860
you can use that result
for a number of purposes.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:55.350
You can use it to apply deploy
time policies, for example,

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:57.750
to only deploy things that
meets a certain vulnerability

00:16:57.750 --> 00:16:58.920
requirement.

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:04.109
You could use it for
human knowledge gathering.

00:17:04.109 --> 00:17:05.819
If you have a
problem and you would

00:17:05.819 --> 00:17:08.230
like to know what are the
vulnerabilities in your running

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:10.690
jobs, you could go
back and take a look

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:15.170
at all of the scanning results.

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:19.111
Last lesson, enforce
image deployment policy--

00:17:19.111 --> 00:17:20.569
so that's what I
mentioned earlier.

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:24.050
Don't blindly trust your
production image repository.

00:17:24.050 --> 00:17:28.440
Instead, verify every image
at the time of deployment.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:30.590
So a couple things
to ensure here

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:33.920
is making sure that you will
deploy images that are actually

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:35.150
viewed by you.

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:39.500
You don't have a developer
downloading a BitMiner job

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:43.370
from the internet directly and
deploy it to your production

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:45.410
environment, for example.

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:47.420
And many of us run
third party images.

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:50.540
So you want to make sure you
have an explicit whitelist

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:54.350
for the search party images that
is allowed on your production

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:57.060
infrastructure.

00:17:57.060 --> 00:17:59.400
Deploy using digest
instead of tags.

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:02.910
Because digests are
content addressable.

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:07.050
And you may be able to round--
it doesn't have the risk

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:10.830
to run into risk conditions,
such as if the tag gets updated

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:13.750
halfway through a
deployment rollout,

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:18.210
it also makes it easy to
examine what happened later on.

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:19.247
You deployed something.

00:18:19.247 --> 00:18:21.330
You want to go back and
see exactly what happened.

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:28.060
Deployments and digests
makes it much easier.

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:30.780
We'll also recommend that
you have deployed policies

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:33.360
on a per production
environment basis

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:35.550
to maximize your flexibility.

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:38.760
If you want to have security
controls for your production

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:41.310
environment, you may have
very strict rules there

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:45.670
that says only deploy things
that is vulnerability free.

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:48.570
Only deploy things
that has QA sign-off.

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:51.240
But for your dev clusters or
for your staging clusters,

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:54.310
you may have more
lenient policies.

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:56.760
As long as it's viewed
by me, I'm OK to deploy.

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:59.550
So it's good to have
those type of policies

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:03.600
be declared as the
runtime environment level.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:08.850
It's also very convenient to
demonstrate security controls

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:13.180
to stakeholders or potentially
compliance officers

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:15.570
to explain to them,
here are the criterias

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:18.078
to apply to each
of my environment.

00:19:18.078 --> 00:19:19.620
And we're going to
demonstrate to you

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:21.410
in one place in a policy.

00:19:24.250 --> 00:19:27.610
It's OK to allow human
to break this process

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:29.210
when there is emergencies.

00:19:29.210 --> 00:19:32.500
But the trick there
is to always log.

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:33.610
There is a structure.

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:36.610
And humans wants to
deploy a quick fix

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:38.560
because things are on fire.

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:40.300
And if we really
don't have time to run

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:42.280
through the entire
pipeline, fine.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:45.790
But log that and so that
you can review that later.

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:50.520
Also, for our vendor
images, we recommend

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:53.460
that you deploy those directly
from cloud marketplace.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:56.370
So you know that those are
authentic images coming

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:58.940
directly from the vendor.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:03.022
So for this lesson, we have a
binary authorization product

00:20:03.022 --> 00:20:03.980
that will help with it.

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:07.640
It's also currently
in beta on GCP.

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:09.080
So what binary
authorization does

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:13.410
is it integrates with different
stages in your CI/CD pipeline.

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:16.490
So as the image passes
through each of the stage,

00:20:16.490 --> 00:20:20.330
it requires a signature
signifying that the image has

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:22.670
passed that particular stage.

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:26.210
And by the time the image gets
to be deployed to production,

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:28.610
final authorization is
integrated to the GKE

00:20:28.610 --> 00:20:31.250
deployment API to
check that does

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:34.130
this image have all
the required signatures

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:37.270
that customer has
set in the policy.

00:20:37.270 --> 00:20:40.820
So this way, even if you have
a privileged individual that

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:42.470
is trying to bypass
your pipeline,

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:44.720
even though that he
has the permission

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:47.300
to deploy to your
production environment,

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:51.260
he will not be able to because
the code that he is deploying

00:20:51.260 --> 00:20:56.210
does not have the
necessary signature on it.

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:59.630
And any failed attempt to
deploy to a binary solution

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:01.970
enabled cluster
gets auto logged.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:05.700
So you can review all
the events later on.

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:07.390
In addition to
signature verification,

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:10.190
we also support
explicit whitelisting.

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:12.530
So your security
admin can maintain

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:17.750
a centralized third party
image whitelist that says,

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:20.430
I only trust this
version of an NGX.

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:25.580
I only trust this version
of a Ruby on Rails.

00:21:25.580 --> 00:21:27.560
And if you have a
developer that is trying

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:31.580
to use it accidentally or
maliciously deploy something

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:34.720
that is older, outdated,
or has vulnerability,

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:37.490
he would not be able
to because, again, it's

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:41.260
not explicitly whitelisted.

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:45.940
So I told you about four best
practices and a few products

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:48.400
to help with the best practice.

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:50.560
But here it is
all tied together.

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:53.180
You have one
centralized pipeline.

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:57.040
And you can use a
secure minimum base

00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:00.820
image that feed into
your centralized builder.

00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:03.820
Once your image gets
checked into the repository,

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:06.620
it gets automatically scanned.

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:10.520
And then it gets deployed
subject to a deploy policy

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:12.380
that you control.

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:14.210
If it passes the
deploy policy, great.

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:15.250
It's a trusted image.

00:22:15.250 --> 00:22:16.910
It goes to production.

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:20.390
If not, untrusted image
gets logged into auto log

00:22:20.390 --> 00:22:23.690
and for future review.

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:26.170
And here are the
three features on GCPs

00:22:26.170 --> 00:22:29.260
that I told you about that
are available for usage now.

00:22:29.260 --> 00:22:33.940
We'll have a Google-managed,
secure, minimal base image

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:38.140
that gets updated continuously
that you can just directly pull

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:39.515
that into your computer.

00:22:39.515 --> 00:22:41.890
It will have a
vulnerability scanning

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:44.470
that's baked into
the Google Container

00:22:44.470 --> 00:22:46.390
Registry that
happens automatically

00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:48.520
for every image you check in.

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:52.090
We have a binary authorization
to enforce this deployed policy

00:22:52.090 --> 00:22:55.210
to your runtime environment to
make sure that only images you

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:59.010
trust get to go in there.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:03.580
And now Vic will show you
how the different pieces

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:05.083
tie together in action.

00:23:05.083 --> 00:23:07.250
VIC IGLESIAS: All right,
let's give it up for Sandra

00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:09.440
for dropping some
knowledge right there.

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:09.970
Yes.

00:23:09.970 --> 00:23:10.900
SANDRA GUO: Thank you.

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:11.983
VIC IGLESIAS: Spectacular.

00:23:11.983 --> 00:23:14.050
If we could switch over to--

00:23:14.050 --> 00:23:16.810
all right, so I'm going to demo
a lot of what Sandra talked

00:23:16.810 --> 00:23:20.140
about as far as best practices
through a CI/CD pipeline

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:21.490
with a sample application.

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:23.708
In my case, I have a frontend
and a backend service

00:23:23.708 --> 00:23:26.000
that I'm going to deploy
independently into Kubernetes.

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:28.390
And it's going to go
through this pipeline,

00:23:28.390 --> 00:23:30.460
very similar to what
Sandra just showed.

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:32.418
So we're going to have
a developer environment.

00:23:32.418 --> 00:23:34.210
Then we're going to
commit some code that's

00:23:34.210 --> 00:23:35.500
going to be picked up by SI.

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:36.580
We're going to
create some images

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:38.388
and some Kubernetes
manifests that are then

00:23:38.388 --> 00:23:40.180
going to be deployed
by Spinnaker, which is

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:41.680
our continuous deployment tool.

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:43.900
You'll notice we have
two different tools

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:46.227
and two different identities
for our CI and CD here.

00:23:46.227 --> 00:23:47.810
SANDRA GUO: This is
lesson number one,

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:49.662
centralized lockdown.

00:23:49.662 --> 00:23:51.370
VIC IGLESIAS: She's
got all the policies.

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:53.328
She told me how to
build this thing.

00:23:53.328 --> 00:23:55.120
So then we're going to
deploy into staging,

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:57.640
and then have an approval,
and go into production.

00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:01.150
So as far as the logical view of
the CI/CD pipeline, where we're

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:03.820
going to bake in some
checks is to make sure

00:24:03.820 --> 00:24:06.470
after we trigger our commit,
that we pass unit tests.

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:08.470
So we don't want any code
going into production

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:09.550
that hasn't passed unit tests.

00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:12.190
So this is where we're going to
add one of those check points

00:24:12.190 --> 00:24:16.120
that the binary authorization
API lets us add.

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:18.520
And we're going to add that
for our passing unit tests.

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:19.990
Once it's passed
unit tests, we're

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:21.448
going to build our
container image,

00:24:21.448 --> 00:24:24.280
push it to the
container registry,

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:26.530
and in there, it's
automatically going to kick off

00:24:26.530 --> 00:24:28.190
a vulnerability scan.

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:31.673
The next thing we're going to
do is add a attestation or one

00:24:31.673 --> 00:24:33.340
of these signatures
that says that there

00:24:33.340 --> 00:24:36.940
were no critical
vulnerabilities in that image.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:39.600
Once that's been
detected by Spinnaker,

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:41.100
it's going to deploy
at the staging,

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:42.680
and we're going to add
another signature there,

00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:44.792
another attestation that
says that we've actually

00:24:44.792 --> 00:24:46.750
passed through the staging
process and somebody

00:24:46.750 --> 00:24:49.100
has clicked this is good to go.

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:51.802
And then we're going to go ahead
and deploy into production.

00:24:51.802 --> 00:24:53.260
Through this demo,
we're also going

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:55.610
to try to bypass our
security in two ways.

00:24:55.610 --> 00:24:58.710
One is by committing some
vulnerable code as a developer,

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:00.200
this can happen to anybody.

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:02.920
And then the other one is
having our credentials phishes,

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:04.660
maybe through some
social engineering,

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:05.260
or something like that.

00:25:05.260 --> 00:25:07.177
Or just, you left your
laptop open and someone

00:25:07.177 --> 00:25:08.458
can access your credentials.

00:25:08.458 --> 00:25:10.000
We're going to try
to deploy directly

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:11.230
into our production cluster.

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:13.840
We'll see what that looks like.

00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:17.470
All right, so I have
a Cloud Shell here.

00:25:17.470 --> 00:25:19.840
I have my source code available.

00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:20.710
It's a going app.

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:24.010
Like I said before, it's got
a backend and a frontend.

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:28.180
And I'm just going to run a
command called skaffold dev.

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:30.760
And that's going to
actually build my container,

00:25:30.760 --> 00:25:32.950
push it to the registry,
and then deploy

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:38.720
my manifest into my GKE cluster
that I'm using for development.

00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:40.850
So here I can see that
skaffold has deployed.

00:25:40.850 --> 00:25:42.850
It's actually doing some
port forwarding for me,

00:25:42.850 --> 00:25:45.590
so I should be able to
access it directly from here.

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:46.090
Cool.

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:48.100
So this is my
development version.

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:50.260
We're going to try to walk
through the happy path

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:51.177
of the CI/CD pipeline.

00:25:51.177 --> 00:25:52.593
So let's say that
I'm a developer,

00:25:52.593 --> 00:25:55.180
and I want to change from blue
to green because some product

00:25:55.180 --> 00:25:57.860
manager decided that
green is in right now.

00:25:57.860 --> 00:26:00.460
And so what we're going
to notice down here

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:03.080
is skaffold has detected that
I've changed my source code.

00:26:03.080 --> 00:26:04.300
And it's going to
do that loop again.

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:05.560
It's going to rebuild my code.

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:07.420
It's going to push
out an image and then

00:26:07.420 --> 00:26:10.150
redeploy into my development
Kubernetes cluster.

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:11.575
That's just completed.

00:26:11.575 --> 00:26:13.860
And now it's going to do
the port forwarding again.

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:16.540
And we should be able to see
our changes show up shortly

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:20.020
inside of our dev environment.

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:22.690
There we go.

00:26:22.690 --> 00:26:23.260
There we go.

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:23.760
Great.

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:25.270
So green-- green looks great.

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:26.770
Let's go ahead and
commit this code.

00:26:31.423 --> 00:26:32.006
Make it green.

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:36.610
And push it.

00:26:36.610 --> 00:26:38.110
And as soon as I
push it, it's going

00:26:38.110 --> 00:26:39.443
to be detected by our CI system.

00:26:39.443 --> 00:26:42.450
In this case, we're using
Cloud build, which is

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:44.940
going to detect a git commit.

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:47.750
Let's go ahead and
see if that's started.

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:48.250
Great.

00:26:48.250 --> 00:26:52.155
So it detected a change
to my backend image.

00:26:52.155 --> 00:26:53.280
And it started the process.

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:54.950
And here, what it's
going to do is first

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:56.855
build the container image.

00:26:56.855 --> 00:26:58.730
It's going to have it
locally, and then we're

00:26:58.730 --> 00:26:59.690
going to start tests.

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:02.120
And as we're testing it, it's
going to push that image up

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.282
to our Container Registry.

00:27:04.282 --> 00:27:05.990
When it gets into the
Container Registry,

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:09.320
it's automatically going to
kick off a vulnerability scan.

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:11.150
And in the meantime,
we're also going

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:13.220
to add our attestation,
our signature that

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:15.290
says we have passed
unit tests if we

00:27:15.290 --> 00:27:16.920
have gone through these phases.

00:27:16.920 --> 00:27:20.810
So we can see here, the build
has completed or is about to.

00:27:20.810 --> 00:27:24.660
And then we're going to go
ahead and push that image.

00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:27.460
While that's happening, we
can go ahead and check out

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:30.320
what our binary authorization
policies look like.

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:32.680
Sandra talked about
having policies

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:35.960
centralized for our various
projects and clusters.

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:38.410
So here I can define my policy.

00:27:38.410 --> 00:27:40.960
If we go into the
edit pane here,

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:43.820
we can see that by
default in this project,

00:27:43.820 --> 00:27:47.500
I allow all images, but then
I have cluster specific rules

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:48.430
that I can add.

00:27:48.430 --> 00:27:50.710
Another thing that I can
do is have a dry run mode,

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:53.530
where I add a pretty
enforcing policy.

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:54.910
But I don't actually
reject them.

00:27:54.910 --> 00:27:58.300
I just log into
Stackdriver login.

00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:01.700
So for my production cluster,
I have a specific rule here.

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:03.970
And that's going
to say that I have

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:07.300
to have these signatures
of these attestations added

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:11.270
into my image before it can
be deployed into this cluster.

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:12.890
And that is my
production cluster.

00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:16.030
So I have to make sure that
I have my unit tests passed,

00:28:16.030 --> 00:28:19.720
my vulnerabilities are checked
and there are no critical ones,

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:23.650
and that I've passed through
the staging manual operation.

00:28:23.650 --> 00:28:25.930
So that's my kind
of default policy.

00:28:25.930 --> 00:28:27.183
I can also add exemptions.

00:28:27.183 --> 00:28:28.600
And one of the
exemptions I can do

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:31.300
is say, anything that's
provided by Google as part of us

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:33.680
running your GKE
clusters, for example,

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:34.840
we're just going to allow.

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:38.140
And that's this
list of images here.

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:41.490
But I also added one for a thing
that I added to my cluster.

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:43.170
In this case, I
added calico, and I

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:45.760
want to make sure that calico
can run inside that cluster.

00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:49.758
So I whitelisted that
particular path for images.

00:28:49.758 --> 00:28:51.050
Let's go ahead and take a look.

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:53.550
OK, so CI has passed,
and now we should

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:55.360
be able to go back
and check our images.

00:28:55.360 --> 00:28:55.860
Whoops.

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:01.500
There we go.

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:04.390
So here's my backend image.

00:29:04.390 --> 00:29:07.420
All right, so I just uploaded
that image and ran the scan.

00:29:07.420 --> 00:29:09.730
And there was no
vulnerabilities found.

00:29:09.730 --> 00:29:13.990
In here, Spinnaker has picked
up that that image was created

00:29:13.990 --> 00:29:15.430
or was pushed.

00:29:15.430 --> 00:29:18.310
And it started to check that
vulnerability scan result.

00:29:18.310 --> 00:29:21.160
And this is where you can
start to, in automation,

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:23.710
interpret the results
of a vulnerability scan.

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:26.950
It's not necessarily
the case that every CVE

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:29.312
is a problem for
your CI/CD process

00:29:29.312 --> 00:29:30.770
or for your particular
application.

00:29:30.770 --> 00:29:33.380
So you might whitelist some
things or things like that.

00:29:33.380 --> 00:29:37.720
In my case, what I do is I set
a threshold for the CVSS score

00:29:37.720 --> 00:29:41.480
that is given to each
CVE that's in an image.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:43.360
And if there are any
that are above five,

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:46.000
I make sure to block the
pipeline from running.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:48.020
And we'll see that
in a little bit.

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:49.990
Now that my vulnerability
check has passed

00:29:49.990 --> 00:29:51.760
and I have no
vulnerabilities that

00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:53.920
are over my threshold,
which was five,

00:29:53.920 --> 00:29:56.510
it should be deploying
into staging.

00:29:56.510 --> 00:29:58.670
And I should see staging
is now rolling out

00:29:58.670 --> 00:30:00.170
with the green version.

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:03.380
As the release manager
and only person,

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:05.060
frankly, running
this pipeline, I

00:30:05.060 --> 00:30:09.530
can go ahead and see that I've
got my manual judgment here.

00:30:09.530 --> 00:30:12.170
And it gives me links to
maybe my metrics dashboard.

00:30:12.170 --> 00:30:14.810
I'm going to check
on some key metrics

00:30:14.810 --> 00:30:18.320
that I need to check for
performance, for example,

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:20.040
the latency of the application.

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:23.135
I can view the sites for
staging and production.

00:30:23.135 --> 00:30:25.260
But I'm just going to
continue because I've already

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:28.460
checked that our staging looked
good, and it had the green.

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:31.250
And then that's going to
add another attestation

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:35.540
that I've actually asserted that
this pipeline can move forward.

00:30:35.540 --> 00:30:38.510
With that, we should have
production showing up

00:30:38.510 --> 00:30:40.780
with green shortly.

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:42.480
Now I wanted to show
what would happen

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:45.970
if I checked in a
vulnerability as a developer.

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:51.190
So let's say that
in my Docker file,

00:30:51.190 --> 00:30:54.297
I'm using Alpine, which
is a pretty minimal image.

00:30:54.297 --> 00:30:55.380
And that's pretty awesome.

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:58.890
But I find that I need some
new library or something that

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:01.270
is not available in
the Alpine image.

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:02.650
So I want to switch to Ubuntu.

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:05.462
And I have an Ubuntu
Docker file that I create.

00:31:05.462 --> 00:31:06.420
I'm using Ubuntu 18.04.

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:08.940
It seems like a
reasonable thing to do.

00:31:08.940 --> 00:31:14.930
And I just change that
here to use the Ubuntu one.

00:31:14.930 --> 00:31:18.510
And that's going to screw me up.

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:19.010
Oh, boy.

00:31:21.820 --> 00:31:25.520
Typing on this thing can be
difficult. OK, there we go,

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:26.630
Ubuntu.

00:31:26.630 --> 00:31:27.340
Great.

00:31:27.340 --> 00:31:30.040
So now I want to use
that Ubuntu Docker file.

00:31:30.040 --> 00:31:33.070
I've got my change here.

00:31:33.070 --> 00:31:36.970
And I'm going to use that
Ubuntu and push it back

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:38.232
through my pipeline.

00:31:42.970 --> 00:31:43.470
All right.

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:46.470
And as I push that, we'll see
the same kind of flow kick off.

00:31:46.470 --> 00:31:47.970
And we'll notice
one main difference

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:49.637
when we're checking
the vulnerabilities.

00:31:49.637 --> 00:31:51.780
So again, if I go
back here, I should

00:31:51.780 --> 00:31:53.550
see that Cloud
Build has started,

00:31:53.550 --> 00:31:55.320
and it's going to
build my image.

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:58.586
And then we'll see how Spinnaker
reacts differently to that.

00:31:58.586 --> 00:32:02.430
The other type of compromise
that I wanted to show you

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:05.180
and how we would block
that in our cluster

00:32:05.180 --> 00:32:06.930
is if my credentials
were phished.

00:32:06.930 --> 00:32:10.920
And someone wants to
maybe launch a shell

00:32:10.920 --> 00:32:12.820
into my production cluster.

00:32:12.820 --> 00:32:16.350
So let's say I go to
my production cluster.

00:32:16.350 --> 00:32:18.000
And I check out
what pods I have,

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:20.010
which I have access to do.

00:32:20.010 --> 00:32:22.350
I have my frontend and
backend pods in there,

00:32:22.350 --> 00:32:25.410
but I want to run just
a shell using Ubuntu,

00:32:25.410 --> 00:32:29.440
so I say image ubuntu: 16.04.

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:30.510
Great.

00:32:30.510 --> 00:32:31.823
And I'm the hacker man.

00:32:31.823 --> 00:32:33.240
So I'm going to
call it hackerman.

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:35.073
I just want to get a
shell into this cluster

00:32:35.073 --> 00:32:36.810
and poke around a little bit.

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:38.795
So we see here
that the deployment

00:32:38.795 --> 00:32:39.670
was actually created.

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:41.850
So that might be unexpected
for some folks that

00:32:41.850 --> 00:32:44.730
are familiar with Kubernetes.

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:47.550
As we refresh here and
see the workloads that

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:49.770
are in our production
cluster, you

00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:53.320
can see here that hacker man
was actually not allowed.

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:56.280
While the deployment
was created,

00:32:56.280 --> 00:33:00.330
it was actually prevented from
creating any pods because it

00:33:00.330 --> 00:33:01.950
does not have those
at the stations

00:33:01.950 --> 00:33:03.850
that we defined in our policy.

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:07.140
So now we've subverted the
production cluster attack

00:33:07.140 --> 00:33:10.020
by ensuring that we have
our policy set properly

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:11.430
in our project.

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:14.840
Let's go check in on our
Cloud Build setup here.

00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:18.380
Looks like things are
still building and pushing.

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:19.970
Once that's complete,
we should see

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:23.585
it show up in our
vulnerability scans here.

00:33:23.585 --> 00:33:26.270
I'll just go back
and check the images.

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:28.490
While that image is
finishing pushing out,

00:33:28.490 --> 00:33:31.520
we're going to actually look
at what we can see inside

00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:34.610
of Stackdriver logging about
that event of a user actually

00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:36.740
trying to hack into our cluster.

00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:39.480
So in here, I should shortly
see that-- here we go.

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:41.720
So we have a bunch
of events that we

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:46.820
can use to alert maybe off of
this that show that someone

00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:49.970
was trying to run on this
Ubuntu 16.04 in our production

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:50.750
cluster.

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:52.730
And they didn't have all
of these attestations

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:53.580
that we had defined.

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:57.500
So you can use this as a way to
alert yourself of any issues.

00:33:57.500 --> 00:34:00.030
Over here, we should see
the push is complete.

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:04.920
And now we have a
scan that started.

00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:06.920
And I didn't have to do
anything other than turn

00:34:06.920 --> 00:34:08.900
on the vulnerability scan API.

00:34:08.900 --> 00:34:11.900
And now all of my images are
automatically being scanned.

00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:14.750
And shortly, I should
see a nice report

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:16.920
telling me all of the
scans that are in here.

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:22.570
So once that's complete, we
should get a nice report.

00:34:22.570 --> 00:34:24.639
And Spinnaker is probably
already kicked off

00:34:24.639 --> 00:34:26.699
and started to check for
those vulnerabilities.

00:34:26.699 --> 00:34:31.238
So it's actually verifying
that our vulnerabilities are--

00:34:31.238 --> 00:34:32.780
that we have minimal
vulnerabilities.

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:35.860
So you can see here it's waiting
for the vulnerability scan

00:34:35.860 --> 00:34:37.159
to complete.

00:34:37.159 --> 00:34:41.389
If we refresh, it should
have this complete.

00:34:41.389 --> 00:34:41.889
All right.

00:34:41.889 --> 00:34:43.920
So now we can see in
here, a big difference

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:45.449
from our Alpine image.

00:34:45.449 --> 00:34:48.120
The Ubuntu image has
a lot more in it.

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:50.550
We can see that there
are many CVEs in here

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:55.050
and many of them above my
CVSS threshold of five.

00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:59.870
So Spinnaker should detect that
and block this from running.

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:02.410
So if I check out
the output again,

00:35:02.410 --> 00:35:04.150
it should have waited
for it to complete.

00:35:04.150 --> 00:35:05.200
It finished.

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:07.990
And now it lists out all of
the vulnerabilities that I had,

00:35:07.990 --> 00:35:10.450
and I can see that there
are many above 5.o,

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:12.390
which was my threshold.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:14.560
And it's blocked my pipeline.

00:35:14.560 --> 00:35:17.670
So in this demo, you've
seen CI/CD pipeline

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:21.720
with various elements using
both vulnerability scanning

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:24.060
and binary authorization
to lock down

00:35:24.060 --> 00:35:27.390
what can go into a
production environment.

00:35:27.390 --> 00:35:31.760
If we can go back to slides
here, Sandra, I think you're--

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:32.520
SANDRA GUO: Yep.

00:35:32.520 --> 00:35:34.530
VIC IGLESIAS: All right.

00:35:34.530 --> 00:35:39.710
SANDRA GUO: Yeah, Victor
showed us the lessons and tools

00:35:39.710 --> 00:35:41.400
in action.

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:43.260
So what are the takeaways?

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:45.940
What are the quick things that
you can get started and enable

00:35:45.940 --> 00:35:48.040
in pipeline tonight?

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:48.570
Right?

00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:51.570
First, you can minimize
access to your CI/CD pipeline.

00:35:51.570 --> 00:35:54.680
Get rid of the people that
really don't need access

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:56.450
to your CI/CD
pipeline so that you

00:35:56.450 --> 00:35:59.370
have a minimum set of people.

00:35:59.370 --> 00:36:04.490
And if you have time,
separate the service

00:36:04.490 --> 00:36:07.070
account you use for
CI from CD so you

00:36:07.070 --> 00:36:12.422
have some segregation
of responsibilities

00:36:12.422 --> 00:36:13.130
in your pipeline.

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:17.780
Have minimal permissions
set up there.

00:36:17.780 --> 00:36:20.150
But at least minimize the
number of people having

00:36:20.150 --> 00:36:22.640
access to your CI/CD pipeline.

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:28.430
Second, if you haven't yet, use
a Google managed secure base

00:36:28.430 --> 00:36:34.050
image as your base image
in your built pipeline.

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:38.810
So instead of using Alpine
directly or Ubuntu directly,

00:36:38.810 --> 00:36:41.150
using the Google
managed version of it

00:36:41.150 --> 00:36:44.300
so that it gets automatically
updated and patched every time

00:36:44.300 --> 00:36:46.380
you push something
new to production.

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:51.960
It's built using the
latest safe version.

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:53.590
Turn on GCR
vulnerability scanning.

00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:54.590
This is the easiest one.

00:36:54.590 --> 00:36:56.240
It's literally just one click.

00:36:56.240 --> 00:36:58.420
If you use Google
Container Registry,

00:36:58.420 --> 00:37:02.540
turn it on and see all the scary
vulnerabilities start spilling

00:37:02.540 --> 00:37:06.530
out and telling you
of all the bad things

00:37:06.530 --> 00:37:09.460
that are in your image.

00:37:09.460 --> 00:37:11.750
Now at least you have the
information to process

00:37:11.750 --> 00:37:14.480
and potentially act on.

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:17.367
Last, enforce a
builder signature

00:37:17.367 --> 00:37:18.450
with banner authorization.

00:37:18.450 --> 00:37:21.680
So this is sort of the
simplest deploy policy

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:22.850
that you can have.

00:37:22.850 --> 00:37:25.430
Make sure that the
image you deploy

00:37:25.430 --> 00:37:28.680
is actually built by you.

00:37:28.680 --> 00:37:30.110
All right.

00:37:30.110 --> 00:37:32.600
So here are some
additional resources

00:37:32.600 --> 00:37:35.510
to learn more about the
tools and best practices

00:37:35.510 --> 00:37:37.910
that we talked about today.

00:37:37.910 --> 00:37:41.210
The slides will be
online at some point

00:37:41.210 --> 00:37:43.550
after the presentation
so you don't have

00:37:43.550 --> 00:37:45.260
to memorize a short length.

00:37:47.790 --> 00:37:50.730
And here are some additional
container security talks

00:37:50.730 --> 00:37:54.810
at Next that you can go check
out today and the next two

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:55.410
days.

00:37:55.410 --> 00:37:59.660
[MUSIC PLAYING]

